{
    "summary": "Adds item returns with restocking, partial and asynchronously settled (pending/settled) refunds, and a store-credit journal that can pay orders and receive refunds, wired through the API, webhook, mail and order status model.",
    "findings": [
        {
            "dimension": "CORRECTNESS",
            "severity": "HIGH",
            "location": "src/returns.ts:91-94",
            "finding": "\"`restockLines`\" starts its loop at \"`i = 1`\", so the first line of every return is never put back into stock, and a one-line return restocks nothing."
        },
        {
            "dimension": "CORRECTNESS",
            "severity": "HIGH",
            "location": "src/mail.ts:76-79",
            "finding": "\"`sendReturnConfirmation`\" sets the recipient to \"`to: order.id`\" when it should be \"`order.customer.email`\". \"`queueMail`\" rejects that address every time, and the surrounding \"`catch`\" only logs the error, so no return confirmation is ever sent."
        },
        {
            "dimension": "PROPAGATION",
            "severity": "HIGH",
            "location": "src/admin.ts:42-48",
            "finding": "\"`refundedTotal`\" now counts only settled refunds, but \"`adminRefund`\" still uses it as its limit and calls \"`issueRefund`\" without going through \"`requestRefund`\"/\"`refundable`\". While earlier refunds are still pending, a goodwill refund can push the order total past what was paid, which breaks the README rule that refunds never exceed it."
        },
        {
            "dimension": "CORRECTNESS",
            "severity": "HIGH",
            "location": "src/returns.ts:67-73",
            "finding": "\"`returnAmount`\" rounds each line separately with \"`Math.round(... * order.total / order.subtotal)`\", so the lines need not add up to \"`order.total`\". Example: three 10.00 lines on a 20.00 discounted total give 3 x 6.67 = 20.01. A full return then exceeds \"`refundable`\" and is rejected, or it pays back slightly too little. This breaks the README promise that sending back everything pays back exactly what was paid."
        },
        {
            "dimension": "ROBUSTNESS",
            "severity": "HIGH",
            "location": "src/credit.ts:48-54",
            "finding": "\"`spendCredit`\" reads \"`balance`\" before \"`await book(...)`\" and writes \"`balance - amount`\" afterwards. Two spends running at the same time can both pass the balance check (double spend), and a grant that lands in between is overwritten. The comment at line 30 says the booking service answers asynchronously, so this race is real."
        },
        {
            "dimension": "ROBUSTNESS",
            "severity": "MEDIUM",
            "location": "src/webhook.ts:38-40",
            "finding": "\"`isRepeat`\" compares only \"`order.status === event.status`\", so a redelivered \"`refunded`\" event for a \"`partially_refunded`\" order gets past it. \"`settleRefund`\" then throws \"already settled\", and because the provider repeats a delivery until it gets an answer (comment at lines 36-37), it keeps retrying forever. A repeated refund should be detected by \"`refund.status`\"."
        },
        {
            "dimension": "PROPAGATION",
            "severity": "MEDIUM",
            "location": "src/report.ts:14",
            "finding": "\"`revenue`\" still counts only \"`order.status === \"paid\"`\". Any order with even a small partial refund (\"`partially_refunded`\") disappears from the daily revenue completely, when only the refunded part should be deducted."
        }
    ]
}
